= Civil Servants' Strike

Though declaring a new, revolutionary form of government in the people's commissariats, in practice each commissariat has inevitably had to inherit the majority of its personnel, infrastructure, and resources from the previous bourgeois ministries of the same name. While positions all across the political spectrum could be found among the old staff, it's safe to say they generally lean conservative as a whole, and in either case are unappreciative of recent developments. Shocked by the sudden arrest of the Provisional Government, our refusal to compromise with the other parties, or any number of other actions taken in the past few days, the civil servants have launched a mass strike in protest. Commissariat offices are discovered virtually empty, as the experienced administrators, bureaucrats, and white-collar workers leave their new Bolshevik bosses to fend for themselves. 

There are mass shutdowns at railway stations, banks, telegraph offices, law courts, schools, and other vital institutions. Other locations remain open but at greatly diminished capacity, as employees find ways to subtly hinder, disobey, or sabotage the new regime. Our own people are eager, but woefully inexperienced and unprepared for the task ahead of them. 

Needless to say, government functions have plummeted across the board, and without the aid of the old civil servants, we can't expect a return to full capacity any time soon. 

